WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.939
The C++ standard library includes useful random facilities, especially in the <random> header.

2
00:00:04.939 --> 00:00:07.218
The library separates engines from distributions.

3
00:00:07.218 --> 00:00:09.118
Engines generate sequences of values.

4
00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:16.336
Distributions map those values into a shape or range, such as a uniform integer range or a normal distribution.

5
00:00:16.336 --> 00:00:25.074
This design is flexible and powerful for many non-security workloads, but it is easy to misunderstand the boundary between useful randomness and

6
00:00:25.074 --> 00:00:25.834
security randomness.

7
00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:28.493
Most standard engines are deterministic once seeded.

8
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:32.672
That includes common engines used for simulations and general randomized behavior.

9
00:00:32.672 --> 00:00:37.991
Determinism is not a defect in those tools; it is part of their design.

10
00:00:37.991 --> 00:00:45.589
The limitation is that deterministic engines should not be treated as cryptographic random sources for tokens, keys, secrets, or other

11
00:00:45.589 --> 00:00:46.729
security-sensitive values.

12
00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:55.467
A distribution can make output fit a requested range, but it does not create entropy and does not make a deterministic engine unpredictable.

13
00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.367
std::random_device deserves careful treatment.

14
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.546
On some implementations it may be backed by a nondeterministic source.

15
00:01:01.546 --> 00:01:04.205
On others, behavior and suitability can vary.

16
00:01:04.205 --> 00:01:12.183
Portable security-sensitive C++ code should not assume that std::random_device alone is the approved answer for every platform and deployment.

17
00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:19.781
Teams should define an approved CSPRNG interface based on operating-system facilities or maintained cryptographic libraries, then use that interface

18
00:01:19.781 --> 00:01:21.681
consistently at security call sites.

19
00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:26.240
The standard tools remain valuable, but their role should match their guarantees.
